### Stage 2: The Filter (File Name, Extension & Size Validation)
We must update your initiateValidation method in VoucherBulkValidationService.java to enforce the strict naming and size rules before the file is saved to the disk. We will change the method signature slightly to accept the MultipartFile directly so we can inspect its size.
Replace your existing initiateValidation method with this fortified version to handle length constraints, multiple extensions, and size limits.
```java
    public String initiateValidation(org.springframework.web.multipart.MultipartFile file, LocalDate postingDate) throws IOException {
        
        String filename = file.getOriginalFilename();
        long fileSize = file.getSize();

        // 1. File Size Validation (Min 10KB, Max 50MB)
        if (fileSize < 10 * 1024 || fileSize > 50 * 1024 * 1024) {
            log.error("UPLOAD_SECURITY_DENIED | Status: File size out of bounds (Size: {} bytes)", fileSize);
            throw new CustomExceptions.FileProcessingException("File size must be between 10KB and 50MB.");
        }

        // 2. Null or Empty Check
        if (filename == null || filename.isBlank()) {
            throw new CustomExceptions.FileProcessingException("Filename cannot be empty.");
        }

        // 3. File Name Length Validation (Max 20 chars including extension)
        if (filename.length() > 20) {
            log.error("UPLOAD_SECURITY_DENIED | Status: Filename exceeds 20 characters");
            throw new CustomExceptions.FileProcessingException("File name length cannot exceed 20 characters.");
        }

        // 4. Multiple Extension Check (Prevents attacks like 'malicious.php.xlsx')
        if (filename.indexOf('.') != filename.lastIndexOf('.')) {
            log.error("UPLOAD_SECURITY_DENIED | Status: Multiple extensions detected");
            throw new CustomExceptions.FileProcessingException("Files with multiple extensions are strictly prohibited.");
        }

        // 5. File Type Whitelist & Allowed Characters (Alphanumeric, dashes, underscores only)
        if (!filename.matches("^[a-zA-Z0-9_\\-]+\\.(xlsx|xls)$")) {
            log.error("UPLOAD_SECURITY_DENIED | Status: Invalid characters or extension in filename");
            throw new CustomExceptions.FileProcessingException("Invalid file name or type. Only .xls and .xlsx files with alphanumeric names are allowed.");
        }

        String requestId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        
        // 6. Filename change in format which is not guessable (UUID renaming)
        // 7. Temporary Storage with Least Privilege Paths (Keeps path out of user URLs)
        Path tempFile = Files.createTempFile("UPLOAD_" + requestId + "_", ".tmp");

        try (InputStream fileStream = file.getInputStream()) {
            Files.copy(fileStream, tempFile, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            log.error("TEMP_FILE_CREATE_FAIL | ReqID: {} | Status: Disk IO failure", requestId);
            throw new CustomExceptions.FileProcessingException("Failed to secure uploaded file on disk.");
        }

        BulkUploadStateDto state = new BulkUploadStateDto();
        state.setRequestId(requestId);
        state.setStatus("PROCESSING");
        state.setCurrentStage(1);
        state.setMessage("Initializing Upload...");
        state.setTotalRows(0);
        saveStatusToRedis(requestId, state);

        log.info("VALIDATION_INIT | ReqID: {} | Status: Secure storage allocated", requestId);
        self.processAsync(requestId, tempFile.toString(), filename, postingDate);
        
        return requestId;
    }

```
### Stage 3: The Inspector (Content Security & Excel Vulnerabilities)
Because Excel files are technically ZIP archives in disguise, attackers use "Zip Bombs" (decompression attacks) or macro injections to crash servers. Apache POI has safeguards we must enable.
Add this static initialization block to the very top of your VoucherBulkValidationService.java class (right beneath the static regex variables) to globally restrict decompression ratios and block CSV formula injections.
```java
    // ==================================================================================
    // GLOBAL SECURITY CONFIGURATION
    // ==================================================================================
    static {
        // Prevents Zip Bomb / Decompression attacks by enforcing a strict inflation ratio
        org.apache.poi.openxml4j.util.ZipSecureFile.setMinInflateRatio(0.001);
        
        // Prevents massive file memory allocation attacks
        org.apache.poi.openxml4j.util.ZipSecureFile.setMaxEntrySize(100 * 1024 * 1024); // 100MB Max
    }

```
*Note on CSV Injection & XSS:* Your existing code actually already handles this beautifully! Your sanitize(String value) method prepends a single quote (') if a user tries to start a cell with =, +, -, or @. This neutralizes macro and CSV injection attacks natively.
### Stage 4: The Vault (Path Obfuscation & Download Security)
The security requirements mandate that unrestricted file downloads are blocked, and file paths are never exposed in the URL.
Your architecture already successfully hides the disk path by using Redis keys ("FILE_" + reqId + "_ERROR") and fetching the file via the API using a UUID instead of a direct file path.
To ensure strict compliance for the download endpoint in your controller, enforce an explicit Content-Disposition attachment header to force a download rather than allowing the browser to execute the file inline.
```java
    @GetMapping("/download-batch/{batchId}")
    public ResponseEntity<byte[]> downloadBatch(@PathVariable String batchId) {
        byte[] fileData = voucherRequestService.generateBatchExcel(batchId);
        
        return ResponseEntity.ok()
                .header(HttpHeaders.CONTENT_DISPOSITION, "attachment; filename=\"Batch_" + batchId + ".xlsx\"")
                .header(HttpHeaders.CONTENT_TYPE, "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet")
                .header("X-Content-Type-Options", "nosniff") // Forces browser to respect the content type
                .body(fileData);
    }

```
